---
import Guide from '../layouts/Guide.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import VersionNotice from '../components/VersionNotice.astro';
import { release, packetExample } from '../data/site';
---
<Guide title="Choose your replay" description="Use fresh sessions to test application behavior. Use stateless replay to send exactly the recorded traffic." sections={[{id:'inspect',label:'Inspect first'},{id:'live',label:'Fresh live sessions'},{id:'packets',label:'Recorded packets'},{id:'results',label:'Read the result'},{id:'versions',label:'Command versions'}]}>
  <VersionNotice />
  <h2 id="inspect">Start with an offline inspection</h2><Code code={"livewire check issue.pcap -details"} label="Understand the recording" /><p>Check the detected protocols, available sessions, missing traffic, and security requirements. A capture may contain many exchanges; select the one relevant to the problem before opening a live connection.</p>
  <h2 id="live">Live: fresh, stateful sessions</h2><p><code>live</code> recovers application requests, opens a fresh connection, and reacts to live responses through supported adapters. The OS maintains TCP sequence state; a new TLS handshake establishes new keys.</p><Code code={"livewire live issue.pcap -session tcp-0 -t 192.0.2.50 -dry-run\nlivewire live issue.pcap -session tcp-0 -t 192.0.2.50"} label="Preview, then run" /><p>Replace <code>tcp-0</code> with a session ID reported by your capture inspection. The target is your test system; these documentation addresses are examples.</p><h3>Repeat an intermittent issue</h3><Code code={"livewire live issue.pcap -t 192.0.2.50 -n 5"} label="Five fresh attempts" /><p>Each attempt creates new connection state. Inspect per-attempt differences and incomplete exchanges; an aggregate pass count can hide intermittent behavior.</p><h3>Test a specific fault</h3><Code code={"livewire live issue.pcap -t 192.0.2.50 -response-timeout 5s -expect-fault timeout"} label="Expect a response timeout" /><p>The timeout or reset expectation is recorded separately from response equivalence. Fault expectations apply to supported framed TCP/TLS application response reads, after a request is sent.</p><h3>Resume completed work</h3><Code code={"livewire live issue.pcap -t 192.0.2.50 -state-dir ./run-01\nlivewire live issue.pcap -t 192.0.2.50 -resume ./run-01"} label="Durable progress · supported generic routes" /><p>Resume confirmed completed boundaries with the same capture and replay options. Connections, credentials, TLS sessions, and uncertain side effects are not restored. See the <a href="/reference/reliability/">durable replay limits</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="packets">Stateless: recorded packets on the wire</h2><Code code={packetExample} label={`Packet replay · v${release.version}`} /><p>This sends the captured frame bytes in capture order. It does not adapt captured addresses, create a fresh TCP/TLS session, or check replies. Choose the interface explicitly; the network receives the recorded destinations and payloads.</p><Code code={`livewire ${release.packetCommand} -in issue.pcap -i eth0 -pps 100 -n 3`} label="Explicit rate · three passes" /><p>Without a rate override, replay follows captured timing. <code>-pps</code> uses a packet rate; <code>-mbps</code> uses a bit rate. Choose one pacing control. Do not infer fresh-session behavior from a successful packet send.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Encrypted bytes remain encrypted bytes.</strong>Stateless replay can send captured TLS records, but those records are bound to the original connection. For meaningful application replay over fresh TLS, use <code>live</code> with matching recovery material.</div>
  <h2 id="results">Read the evidence</h2><div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Replay outcomes"><table><thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>What it establishes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Matched checked responses</td><td>Positive response evidence matched the selected comparison rules.</td></tr><tr><td>Different</td><td>Observed responses diverged from those rules.</td></tr><tr><td>Incomplete</td><td>The exchange did not finish or required evidence is missing.</td></tr><tr><td>Sent, unverified</td><td>Recorded frames were transmitted. Target behavior was not verified.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="margin-top:20px">A response match does not itself prove the original device issue recurred. Keep fault observations, report details, and target diagnostics together.</p>
  <h2 id="versions">Command names across versions</h2><div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Command version contract"><table><thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Published v{release.version}</th><th>{release.contract === 'legacy-dual-application' ? 'Planned corrected contract' : 'Current contract'}</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>live</code></td><td>Fresh application sessions</td><td>Fresh application sessions</td></tr><tr><td><code>reproduce</code></td><td>{release.contract === 'legacy-dual-application' ? 'Fresh application sessions' : 'Stateless recorded packets'}</td><td>Stateless recorded packets</td></tr><tr><td><code>replay</code></td><td>Stateless recorded packets</td><td>Compatibility alias for reproduce</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="margin-top:20px">Always check <code>livewire version</code> before reusing a command from another release. The examples on this site are bound to the version shown beside the downloads.</p><p class="reference-link"><a href="/reference/commands/">Full command reference →</a> · <a href="/reference/workflow/">Session selection and planning →</a></p>
</Guide>
